cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  FrugalMind: a cost-aware, rigor-preserving evaluation framework for
  scientific AI agents
message: >-
  If you use this framework, or the concepts it introduces (frugality-first
  evaluation against quality floors, the scorability spectrum, negative-case
  discipline, skill-conditioned evaluation and skill lift, the three-family
  taxonomy of scientific evals, or contamination control by secret generator
  seed), please cite the framework paper.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Marine
    family-names: Denolle
    affiliation: >-
      Department of Earth and Space Sciences, University of Washington
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1610-2250'
repository-code: 'https://github.com/mdenolle/frugalmind'
abstract: >-
  FrugalMind is an evaluation framework for scientific AI agents built on the
  premise that the decision a laboratory faces is not "is the model good" but
  "what is the cheapest configuration that clears my scientific quality floor,
  and am I permitted to run it on my data". It treats cost as a first-class
  axis, measures the deployed (model, scaffolding) pair rather than the model,
  verifies against references rather than judgements, treats negative cases as
  first-class, and holds out a hidden test split whose generator is public but
  whose seed is secret.
keywords:
  - AI for science
  - evaluation
  - benchmark
  - scientific agents
  - cost-aware evaluation
  - geoscience
  - seismology
license: MIT

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Attribution model
#
# Framework authorship and eval authorship are recorded SEPARATELY. The
# framework — the concepts listed in `message` above — is the contribution of
# the author(s) listed here. Individual evaluations contributed to the hub carry
# their own authors, their own versioned skill, and their own citable dataset,
# and are credited as such on the leaderboard and in `preferred-citation` of the
# eval's own record. Contributing an eval does not confer authorship of the
# framework, and using the framework does not confer authorship of an eval.
#
# This is what lets the benchmark grow by community contribution without either
# diluting contributors or absorbing them.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

